WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:02.835
-[Rapping] ♪ You thought Bernie
could save you, ho ♪

00:00:02.835 --> 00:00:04.404
♪ Pay back all them dollars
I gave you, ho ♪

00:00:04.404 --> 00:00:06.239
♪ Don't forget
this is a business ♪

00:00:06.239 --> 00:00:08.941
♪ Keep praying
for that forgiveness ♪

00:00:08.941 --> 00:00:10.576
♪ 20 years 'fore you finish ♪

00:00:10.576 --> 00:00:12.912
♪ And then boom, I hit you
with that interest ♪

00:00:12.912 --> 00:00:14.980
♪ But getting an education's
the American dream ♪

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:16.182
♪ And I'm that bitch,
Sallie Mae ♪

00:00:16.182 --> 00:00:18.684
♪ I want to so some schemes ♪

00:00:18.684 --> 00:00:20.419
♪ Do some schemes ♪
Come with a hook right there.

00:00:20.419 --> 00:00:23.189
You say,
♪ Schools and schemes ♪

00:00:23.189 --> 00:00:24.858
♪ Schools ♪

00:00:24.858 --> 00:00:27.093
-Is you rapping
about student loans?

00:00:27.093 --> 00:00:28.527
-Welcome back to "Late Night."

00:00:28.527 --> 00:00:29.929
We're here with Issa Rae.

00:00:29.929 --> 00:00:31.230
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:34.667
So this is your wonderful
new show.

00:00:34.667 --> 00:00:35.935
It is called "Rap [Bleep]."

00:00:35.935 --> 00:00:37.236
-How are you saying it
right now?

00:00:37.236 --> 00:00:38.738
-"Rap [Bleep]."
-Yeah, how are --

00:00:38.738 --> 00:00:40.040
Is it going to be like
"Rap Bleep"?

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:41.374
-Oh, it's going to be beeped,
yeah.

00:00:41.374 --> 00:00:42.942
We found out earlier today.
-Oh. [Bleep].

00:00:42.942 --> 00:00:44.778
-Yeah, so they'll beep you
as well.

00:00:44.778 --> 00:00:46.712
-Okay. [ Laughs ]
-So you obviously knew

00:00:46.712 --> 00:00:48.548
when you named the show
"Rap [Bleep]"

00:00:48.548 --> 00:00:49.883
that this would be a problem.

00:00:49.883 --> 00:00:52.084
-I did, yes.

00:00:52.084 --> 00:00:54.821
I anticipated that
it was a tentative title,

00:00:54.821 --> 00:00:58.491
and then I talked to HBO legal,
like, what can we get away with,

00:00:58.491 --> 00:01:00.059
because we had an asterisk
in the title

00:01:00.059 --> 00:01:02.495
and then we replaced it
with an exclamation point,

00:01:02.495 --> 00:01:03.730
and they were like,
"It's totally fine."

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:06.031
Like, "TV Guide" can print that.

00:01:06.031 --> 00:01:09.201
And so then it was like, yes,
it's out there,

00:01:09.201 --> 00:01:12.104
but now it's on billboards
and little kids are seeing it.

00:01:12.104 --> 00:01:13.706
And sorry, parents.

00:01:13.706 --> 00:01:14.740
[ Laughter ]

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:16.309
But they can read,
which is great.

00:01:16.309 --> 00:01:17.610
-Yeah!

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:19.278
They can read, that's great.
-That's so good. Yeah.

00:01:19.278 --> 00:01:21.481
-I also feel like that's one of
those words --

00:01:21.481 --> 00:01:23.750
what are -- kids are hearing
that at a pretty young age now.

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:24.984
You know?
-Kids are.

00:01:24.984 --> 00:01:26.886
If they have great parents.

00:01:26.886 --> 00:01:27.954
[ Laughter ]

00:01:27.954 --> 00:01:30.122
-So you've got --
this is about two friends

00:01:30.122 --> 00:01:33.626
who reconnect
to start rapping together.

00:01:33.626 --> 00:01:35.794
What gave you this idea?

00:01:35.794 --> 00:01:38.464
-I am a huge rap fan,

00:01:38.464 --> 00:01:41.267
and this idea has kind of been
gestating for a while.

00:01:41.267 --> 00:01:43.369
I've always wanted to have
something set

00:01:43.369 --> 00:01:44.437
in the music world,

00:01:44.437 --> 00:01:46.272
and this is just such
an exciting time.

00:01:46.272 --> 00:01:48.141
Like, there's so many dope
female rappers

00:01:48.141 --> 00:01:50.009
who are out here killing it.

00:01:50.009 --> 00:01:55.648
And one of my favorite
producers, a male,

00:01:55.648 --> 00:01:58.851
happened to say that all women
rap about now is, like,

00:01:58.851 --> 00:02:01.020
their vaginas. Like,
it's all the same, all the same.

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:03.289
And I was like that's not fair
because men rap

00:02:03.289 --> 00:02:04.991
about the same thing
all the time.

00:02:04.991 --> 00:02:07.159
And so that -- that kind of
double standard

00:02:07.159 --> 00:02:08.827
really interested me.

00:02:08.827 --> 00:02:11.130
-Well, it's really fun because
there is sort of that

00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:13.900
more socially, you know,
issue-oriented rap in this,

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:15.835
and then the other
that you mentioned.

00:02:15.835 --> 00:02:17.203
[ Laughs ]
-Yep.

00:02:17.203 --> 00:02:18.404
-Look, I already said
"Rap [Bleep]."

00:02:18.404 --> 00:02:19.538
I don't want to get beeped
again.

00:02:19.538 --> 00:02:20.540
-Like, I don't know,
I don't want to push it.

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:23.543
-[ Laughs ] It's really fun.

00:02:23.543 --> 00:02:25.244
The women you've cast
are fantastic.

00:02:25.244 --> 00:02:26.445
-They're incredible.

00:02:26.445 --> 00:02:29.482
They're all new actresses,
for the most part.

00:02:29.482 --> 00:02:32.151
It's a new cast,
and they're killing it.

00:02:32.151 --> 00:02:33.319
I'm so proud of them.

00:02:33.319 --> 00:02:35.121
-It also seems like you took it
seriously,

00:02:35.121 --> 00:02:36.489
which as a fan,
you would have to.

00:02:36.489 --> 00:02:37.557
If you're doing a show
about rap,

00:02:37.557 --> 00:02:39.425
you have to have actual
good rap lyrics.

00:02:39.425 --> 00:02:42.528
Does that mean you had to find
television writers who were good

00:02:42.528 --> 00:02:43.929
at writing rap songs,

00:02:43.929 --> 00:02:46.699
or did you actually also hire
rap lyricists?

00:02:46.699 --> 00:02:49.635
-So what's great is we were able
to have a traditional

00:02:49.635 --> 00:02:52.138
writers' room, which was,
you know, really funny,

00:02:52.138 --> 00:02:53.539
brilliant writers.

00:02:53.539 --> 00:02:55.207
Some of them were from Miami.

00:02:55.207 --> 00:02:58.978
And then just to be able to
have, like, authentic rap --

00:02:58.978 --> 00:03:00.312
like, the music has to be good.

00:03:00.312 --> 00:03:01.847
They actually
have to be good rappers.

00:03:01.847 --> 00:03:05.751
We got to employ some of
my favorite rap artists,

00:03:05.751 --> 00:03:08.221
like Pineapple City, Incognita,
Dreezy,

00:03:08.221 --> 00:03:10.222
and they wrote lyrics
for the characters.

00:03:10.222 --> 00:03:13.026
And then we had music camps to
make music for the show.

00:03:13.026 --> 00:03:15.194
And it was
like an intricate process.

00:03:15.194 --> 00:03:17.397
And then, of course,
the two kind of met up.

00:03:17.397 --> 00:03:19.332
And it was just -- it was great.

00:03:19.332 --> 00:03:21.134
I'm really proud of the music
of the show.

00:03:21.134 --> 00:03:22.735
-You were only behind the scenes
on this show?

00:03:22.735 --> 00:03:24.437
-Yes, thank the Lord.
-Are you super happy?

00:03:24.437 --> 00:03:25.771
-Oh, my God, are you kidding me?

00:03:25.771 --> 00:03:28.540
I was writing this show while
filming season five

00:03:28.540 --> 00:03:30.843
of "Insecure."
I was tired of being on camera.

00:03:30.843 --> 00:03:33.812
And I was like, "Where can I --
What can I write where I can get

00:03:33.812 --> 00:03:35.581
out of this city?" Because
I wrote it during quarantine.

00:03:35.581 --> 00:03:38.184
I was like, "Ooh, where do
I want to be next? Ooh."

00:03:38.184 --> 00:03:40.086
If the show gets picked up,
Miami.

00:03:40.086 --> 00:03:42.421
And then it was just like --

00:03:42.421 --> 00:03:46.091
I just get to be behind
the scenes and direct --

00:03:46.091 --> 00:03:49.796
I mean, not literally direct,
but, like, as a producer,

00:03:49.796 --> 00:03:53.366
give notes to the amazing cast
and contribute to the story.

00:03:53.366 --> 00:03:54.800
And so, it was just --
it was a lot of fun.

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:56.369
It was a different muscle.

00:03:56.369 --> 00:03:58.370
-You were still on camera
obviously in other things.

00:03:58.370 --> 00:04:00.773
I just saw the trailer for
B.J. Novak's new movie,

00:04:00.773 --> 00:04:02.641
"Vengeance," that I think comes
out in a couple of weeks.

00:04:02.641 --> 00:04:03.909
Looked like a very fun trailer.

00:04:03.909 --> 00:04:05.011
Was that a good time?

00:04:05.011 --> 00:04:06.645
-That was a great time.

00:04:06.645 --> 00:04:08.480
B.J. Novak is incredible.

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:10.382
You know I've been a fan
of his since "The Office,"

00:04:10.382 --> 00:04:13.018
and his writing, and this is
his directorial debut.

00:04:13.018 --> 00:04:15.154
And it's just such
a smart script.

00:04:15.154 --> 00:04:18.791
It's like, you know, set in
the true crime podcast world,

00:04:18.791 --> 00:04:19.892
which I'm obsessed with.

00:04:19.892 --> 00:04:21.293
-Uh-huh.
-Which sounds terrible to say.

00:04:21.293 --> 00:04:24.530
You know? Like, "Oh, I love
true crime murder podcasts!"

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:26.299
-Yeah. You're like, "Do you like
make-believe crimes?"

00:04:26.299 --> 00:04:28.501
"No, only the true ones."
-No, I want it to be real.

00:04:28.501 --> 00:04:29.869
I like suffering.

00:04:29.869 --> 00:04:31.570
But it's terrible --
It's so bad.

00:04:31.570 --> 00:04:33.405 line:85%
[ Laughter ]

00:04:31.570 --> 00:04:33.405
I'm sorry.

00:04:33.405 --> 00:04:36.876
But the movie kind of explores
that in addition to,

00:04:36.876 --> 00:04:39.311
like, truth in America now.

00:04:39.311 --> 00:04:40.679
And it's just brilliantly done,

00:04:40.679 --> 00:04:41.914
and I'm happy to be
a part of it.

00:04:41.914 --> 00:04:43.315
-Well, I'm so happy
to have you here.

00:04:43.315 --> 00:04:44.484
Thanks so much
for making it back.

00:04:44.484 --> 00:04:46.052
It's such a delight to spend
time with you.

00:04:46.052 --> 00:04:47.186
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:47.186 --> 00:04:50.089
Issa Rae! The first two episodes
of "Rap [Bleep]"

00:04:50.089 --> 00:04:52.591
begin streaming July 21st
on HBO Max.

00:04:52.591 --> 00:04:54.660
And "Vengeance" is in theaters
July 29th.

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:56.095
We'll be right back
with Nikki Glaser.

